     1	# Decision 0004: compile-only build drivers
     2	
     3	## Context
     4	
     5	Schemas 1 through 5 can copy script commands or resolve system commands but
     6	cannot produce a native artifact from source. Allowing a package to supply a
     7	shell command, executable, argument vector, environment, plugin, generator, or
     8	build-system recipe would contradict the existing rule that installation does
     9	not execute package-provided code.
    10	
    11	Compiled artifacts also cannot reuse the existing `skill + commit` runtime
    12	identity. Compiler-visible inputs include files outside one package directory,
    13	the selected native target and policy, and the exact trusted toolchain. An
    14	internally consistent receipt from an attacker-writable cache is not proof that
    15	the manager produced its artifact.
    16	
    17	## Decision
    18	
    19	Manifest schema 6 is a declarative compiled-artifact extension. It adds
    20	explicit `build_roots` and one closed command object containing only
    21	`type: "build"`, `driver: "go-v1"`, and `source_dir`. Build roots are dedicated
    22	link-free module roots below the snapshot, cannot be `.`, cannot overlap
    23	runtime roots, and are statically excluded from agent context and runtime
    24	copying before cache lookup or compiler discovery. The same exclusion applies
    25	to real builds, exact cache hits, and dry-runs. Artifacts live only in
    26	manager-owned staging and immutable cache state.
    27	
    28	Protocol v1 defines only `go-v1`. It is a fixed native, vendor-only,
    29	networkless, cgo-free Go 1.23+ pipeline using an operator-trusted and
    30	fingerprinted release family. The manager owns the process graph, environment,
    31	arguments, target, output path, dependency preflight, directive checks, and
    32	internal-link policy. It rejects non-standard assembly, host objects,
    33	`//go:cgo_import_dynamic`, external linking, libgcc fallback, PGO, workspaces,
    34	toolchain switching, and any executable child outside the fingerprinted Go
    35	tool directory. The built artifact is never executed during manager activity.
    36	
    37	Two source/currentness identities remain separate:
    38	
    39	- installed-tree `content_sha256` retains its schema 1 through 5 semantics and
    40	  excludes the manager marker;
    41	- `curator-build-source-v1` is domain-separated and length-framed over every
    42	  regular file in the validated raw snapshot, including a package-provided
    43	  root `.csk-install.json`, and is computed before cache lookup.
    44	
    45	The build input also binds `curator-go-toolchain-v1`, command/source selection,
    46	native target and tuning, driver revision, and the complete fixed policy. Its
    47	CCJ-1 digest is the logical cache key. Exact canonical receipt bytes bind the
    48	input and manager-derived artifact path/hash/size; their digest supplies
    49	corruption and currentness detection, not authentication. Persistent reuse
    50	requires a separately verified manager-created, owner-protected, link-safe
    51	cache boundary. Untrusted protection forces a rebuild from the revalidated
    52	snapshot, even when every embedded hash matches.
    53	
    54	Marker schema 2 records build roots, build-source identity, logical cache keys,
    55	receipt hashes, artifact hashes, and manager-derived artifact-relative paths.
    56	Managers read marker schemas 1 and 2, write v2 after mutation, and may retain a
    57	valid v1 marker as current for schema 1 through 5 packages. Shims for build
    58	commands select immutable cache artifacts rather than the commit-keyed script
    59	runtime store.
    60	
    61	A real operation validates and builds misses in operation-private staging
    62	before installation mutation. Independent projects may validate and compile
    63	concurrently, but cache publication, project/global/hybrid/adapter mutation,
    64	consumer update, rollback/recovery, and garbage collection serialize beneath
    65	one manager-home mutation lock. One journal covers the ordered mutable target
    66	set; the consumer ledger commits last; rollback stays locked and proceeds in
    67	reverse commit order. This prevents cross-project lost updates and stale
    68	backup restoration.
    69	
    70	Dry-run performs only package-independent toolchain probes and read-only
    71	planning. It does not run `go list`, `go build`, a compiler, or linker; create
    72	Go caches, persistent fingerprint memos, mutation locks, journals, or manager
    73	state; publish/quarantine/repair cache entries; or change agent context,
    74	runtime, markers, shims, adapters, audit/registry state, or consumers.
    75	
    76	Only logical identity and validation are portable. Manager-home directories,
    77	physical cache-root and driver-subdirectory names, receipt filenames, lock and
    78	quarantine names, and storage backends are deliberately non-normative. An
    79	illustrative layout such as `<home>/<cache>/go-v1/<key>/bin/<command>` MUST NOT
    80	be treated as a compatibility identifier.
    81	
    82	## Rejected alternatives
    83	
    84	- Arbitrary package shell commands, executable names, argv/environment arrays,
    85	  output selection, hooks, plugins, generators, tests, and pre/post-build steps
    86	  were rejected because they transfer install-time execution control to the
    87	  package.
    88	- Cargo was rejected for v1 because build scripts and procedural macros execute
    89	  package-selected code. Direct Rust compilation still needs a closed
    90	  dependency and linking contract.
    91	- Zig and Swift were deferred because their build manifests, plugins, macros,
    92	  or compile-time execution require distinct process and side-effect models.
    93	- Make, CMake, Ninja, and Meson were rejected as package-controlled recipe
    94	  frontends rather than fixed compilers.
    95	- Maven, Gradle, MSBuild, and package-oriented Java/Kotlin/.NET compilation were
    96	  deferred because tasks, plugins, annotation processors, analyzers, and source
    97	  generators execute at build time.
    98	- npm lifecycle scripts and Node/TypeScript bundler configuration, loaders, and
    99	  plugins were rejected. A fixed transpiler or Deno subset still needs a closed
   100	  dependency, configuration, and runtime-output model.
   101	- Python package builds were rejected because PEP 517 frontends execute the
   102	  package-selected backend and may resolve dynamic build requirements.
   103	- Direct C/C++ compilers were deferred because a portable driver must own and
   104	  identify source enumeration, preprocessing, sysroots, response files,
   105	  compiler plugins, native dependencies, and linking inputs.
   106	- `skill + commit`, installed-tree `content_sha256`, and receipt-only trust were
   107	  rejected as cache identities because they omit compiler/toolchain policy or
   108	  allow attacker-created self-consistent candidates.
   109	- Standardizing cache directory names and receipt filenames was rejected
   110	  because machine-local layout is an implementation boundary, not wire state.
   111	
   112	## Compatibility impact
   113	
   114	Schema 1 through 5 manifests and marker-v1 currentness retain their exact
   115	deployed behavior. They reject the schema-6-only field and command surface.
   116	Schema 6 and marker v2 are explicit reader/writer version transitions; readers
   117	never infer them from fields. The build feature is released with a new current
   118	protocol `1.0.0-rc.4` conformance claim schema 2. Conformance claim schema 1
   119	remains frozen as historical rc.3 evidence and cannot claim schema-6 or
   120	build-driver behavior.
   121	
   122	## Security impact
   123	
   124	The accepted model preserves the no-hooks invariant while admitting untrusted
   125	compiler input. It does not claim that the Go compiler or compiled artifact is
   126	safe. Compiler-input denial of service and vulnerabilities require host
   127	resource limits and sandboxing, while artifact execution remains a later user
   128	action. Protected local cache state is part of the v1 trusted computing base;
   129	same-principal, administrator/root, kernel, and hostile-storage compromise are
   130	outside that boundary and require cache reuse to be disabled when unacceptable.
   131	
   132	## Future-driver rule
   133	
   134	A future driver requires a new closed identifier and independent protocol and
   135	security review. The proposal must define a strict manifest surface, complete
   136	compiler-input boundary, trusted toolchain identity, fixed direct process graph
   137	and environment, network/dependency/link policy, manager-derived output,
   138	logical cache/receipt identity, dry-run behavior, lifecycle/rollback rules, and
   139	cross-language conformance vectors. It MUST demonstrate that package-selected
   140	hooks, plugins, generators, macros, build recipes, and produced output cannot
   141	execute during manager activity. No future driver may be enabled by broadening
   142	`go-v1` or adding a generic fallback.
     1	# Security policy
     2	
     3	## Reporting
     4	
     5	Report suspected vulnerabilities privately to `ivan@relux.works`. Include the
     6	affected protocol section or schema, a minimal reproducer, and the security
     7	impact. Do not include secrets, private registry records, or production keys.
     8	
     9	Acknowledgement is targeted within three business days. Coordinated disclosure
    10	and release timing depend on severity and whether deployed implementations need
    11	updates before publication.
    12	
    13	## Security model
    14	
    15	The protocol treats skill repositories, manifests, registry responses, cache
    16	entries, and bundles as untrusted input. Conforming implementations MUST apply
    17	the parsing limits and validation rules in the normative documents before
    18	using paths, signatures, references, or configuration values.
    19	
    20	Repository control includes source, both manifest spellings, a package-provided
    21	root `.csk-install.json`, `go.mod`, `go.sum`, `vendor/`, build constraints,
    22	compiler directives, embedded inputs, assembly, host objects, filenames, and
    23	file sizes. Build receipts, artifacts, stale markers, and candidate cache bytes
    24	are also untrusted parser and hash input until admitted through the protected
    25	cache boundary below.
    26	
    27	The protocol provides integrity, provenance, revocation, rollback detection,
    28	and deterministic installation. Capability declarations and source auditing
    29	are review and policy surfaces; they are not runtime sandboxes. A successful
    30	audit or registry attestation does not make skill-provided code safe to execute
    31	without the consuming agent's own isolation and authorization controls.
    32	
    33	## Compile-only build boundary
    34	
    35	Manifest schema 6 does not introduce package hooks. It admits only untrusted Go
    36	source as compiler input to the closed, manager-owned `go-v1` process described
    37	in `protocol/core.md`. During install, update, repair, status, garbage
    38	collection, and dry-run, a conforming manager:
    39	
    40	- MUST NOT invoke a package-provided executable, script, shell command,
    41	  interpreter entry point, shared library, test, package-manager hook, build
    42	  backend, or arbitrary build recipe;
    43	- MUST NOT accept or derive package-controlled executable names, argument
    44	  arrays, environment variables, flags, tags, linker/compiler options,
    45	  toolchains, output names or paths, build scripts, pre/post-build actions, or
    46	  output-selection rules;
    47	- MUST NOT load package-produced code as a compiler or manager plugin, macro,
    48	  annotation processor, source generator, loader, or procedural extension;
    49	- MUST NOT use `sh -c`, `cmd.exe /c`, PowerShell, or any other shell to create a
    50	  build command;
    51	- MUST NOT run `go generate`, `go run`, `go test`, `go install`, `go get`, any
    52	  `go mod` mutation/download command, or a package-selected VCS/network helper;
    53	- MUST NOT invoke or fall back to cgo, an external compiler/linker, libgcc
    54	  discovery, package-controlled assembly, host objects, or a dynamic import
    55	  requested by `//go:cgo_import_dynamic` in active non-standard Go source;
    56	- MUST NOT execute the built output for version discovery, smoke testing,
    57	  post-processing, receipt generation, rollback, validation, or any other
    58	  install-time purpose; and
    59	- MAY perform fixed parsing, hashing, copying, permission changes, and schema
    60	  validation in manager code, and MAY pass validated untrusted source bytes to
    61	  the exact operator-trusted `go-v1` compiler pipeline.
    62	
    63	The manager therefore MUST NOT invoke package Make, CMake, Ninja, or Meson
    64	recipes; Cargo build scripts or procedural macros; SwiftPM manifests, plugins,
    65	or macros; Maven or Gradle plugins/tasks; MSBuild tasks or Roslyn generators;
    66	npm lifecycle scripts, Node bundler configuration, loaders, or plugins; Python
    67	PEP 517 build backends; or comparable executable build-system surfaces. There
    68	is no generic-hook fallback when a package cannot fit `go-v1`. A future driver
    69	requires a new closed identifier and independent security review.
    70	
    71	`go-v1` forces internal linking and disables libgcc lookup, cgo, PGO,
    72	workspaces, toolchain switching, non-standard assembly, host objects, and the
    73	active non-standard `//go:cgo_import_dynamic` directive. If the native target
    74	or dependency graph cannot build within that surface, installation fails. A
    75	manager MUST reject a build when it cannot enforce the fixed environment,
    76	network denial, source/context separation, native target, and process graph;
    77	it MUST NOT approximate the contract.
    78	
    79	### Compiler-input trust boundary
    80	
    81	Compile-only does not mean trusted source or an invulnerable compiler. Malicious
    82	source may exploit parser/compiler defects or exhaust CPU, memory, disk,
    83	processes, diagnostic output, or filesystem traversal. The selected Go
    84	toolchain, manager, operating system, hashing/canonicalization code, and
    85	manager-owned policy/staging roots are trusted; package source and compiler
    86	diagnostics are not. Source is read-only to children, standard input is closed,
    87	and stdout/stderr MUST be bounded and redacted before presentation. Managers
    88	MUST apply filesystem and network denial plus time, memory, disk, process-count,
    89	and output limits where the host supports them. The compiled artifact remains
    90	untrusted package code until a user later invokes the installed command under
    91	the consuming environment's authorization and isolation policy.
    92	
    93	### Protected-cache trust boundary
    94	
    95	Receipt and marker SHA-256 values establish deterministic consistency and
    96	currentness; they are not signatures, MACs, registry attestations, or
    97	independent proof that `go-v1` produced an artifact. Persistent reuse is
    98	permitted only inside manager-created state whose ownership, private
    99	permissions or DACL, containment, file type, and link safety are revalidated
   100	on every lookup and before locked publication/commit. A self-consistent receipt
   101	outside that boundary is attacker-controlled and MUST be treated as a miss.
   102	Managers that cannot enforce the boundary MUST disable persistent reuse and
   103	rebuild from the revalidated snapshot; they MUST NOT repair permissions and
   104	adopt pre-existing candidate bytes.
   105	
   106	The v1 boundary assumes an adversary cannot mutate protected state as the
   107	manager's operating-system principal or as a trusted administrator. Arbitrary
   108	same-principal code execution, administrator/root or kernel compromise, and
   109	hostile storage below the operating system are outside this install-time
   110	invariant because they can replace the manager and markers as well as receipts.
   111	Deployments that cannot accept that assumption MUST disable persistent cache
   112	reuse. Authenticated cross-principal artifact provenance would require a future
   113	protocol; a plain receipt hash does not provide it.
   114	
   115	Client snapshot high-water state is security state. It is stored separately
   116	from disposable registry responses, written atomically before acceptance, and
   117	included in protected machine backups. Existing corruption or a failed write
   118	is fail-closed. Loss of all local state still requires an out-of-band
   119	authenticated checkpoint or explicit operator rebootstrap; signatures alone
   120	cannot prove that a newly presented snapshot is not an old valid view.
   121	
   122	Registry trust anchors are distributed out of band. Removing a key from the
   123	pinned set revokes trust in signatures made solely by that key. Key rotation
   124	and incident behavior are defined in `protocol/registry.md`. The production
   125	registry threat model, including replay, rollback, equivocation, cursor abuse,
   126	resource exhaustion, credential compromise, crash recovery, and backup
   127	rollback, is normative in `profiles/registry-service.md`.
   128	
   129	## Release review
   130	
   131	Stable protocol releases require:
   132	
   133	1. schema and vector CI on all supported operating systems;
   134	2. both conforming clients passing the same released vectors;
   135	3. review of changes to canonicalization, hashing, signatures, snapshots,
   136	   transparency logs, source identities, and path handling;
   137	4. a signed release tag and immutable release artifacts.
   138	
   139	`1.0.0-rc.2` is not promoted to stable until an independent security review
   140	and an independent interoperability review conform to
   141	`reviews/review-report-v2.schema.json`. Each report identifies the reviewed
